From 50f40639f205f98a93fd7ea1d5c4addb23193a73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 23:12:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] [output] surface_fluxes: the ocean's net surface fluxes of T
 and S, daily; a 30-day NEMO run with them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

panarctic.jl: with [output] surface_fluxes = true, averages over average_hours of NumericalEarth's
net_fluxes of the child (T, S and the freshwater volume flux η, those that are fields) in
arctic_surface_fluxes.jld2. melt_lens.jl reads it when present: freshening under ice Jˢ / S
(mm/day; negative = brine) and heat loss ρ₀ cₚ Jᵀ (W/m²), table and two figure rows.
config_smoke_test_fluxes.toml (100 km, 4 days) and
config_sep2005_30d_nemo_glorys12_nz100_fluxes.toml (September 2005, as the four-month NEMO run).

Why: under ice the top 10 m has κᶜ of 1–40 m²/s from day 1 (melt_lens.md), which only the enhanced
vertical diffusion (100 m²/s where N² < 0) can give, with e ~1e-4 m²/s² in the top cells: the top
cells are statically unstable much of the time. Brine (salt into the top cell) or cooling?

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AbhhvmHjZYCHhFxumZ9A47
